  "worked_solution": "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-3)=4$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}f(x)=4.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)=-3$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-3.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=-1$ and $x=4,$ but they are not jump discontinuities.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=-3$ and $x=2$ only.",
